@Tutorial(time: 20) {
  @Intro(title: "State, When and Effects (Reducer)") {
    The Reducer counterpart of <doc:01-02-StateWhenAndEffects>: trigger effects that report
    back to your reducer as a new `When` event, with the same synchronous update contract,
    now expressed as a pure static function.
  }

  @Section(title: "What is a side effect?") {
    @ContentAndMedia {
      A side effect is any executed code whose result arrives later, or unclearly. As
      developers we want as few of these as possible, except for one fully expected group:
      asynchronous tasks. We'll work through the most common one, a network request, and
      show how it fits the Reducer's synchronous `update` contract.
    }
    @Steps {
      @Step {
        Let's start from the acceptance criteria, same as always. We'll build a counter
        that's stored remotely via a network API, and the test below is the ACCEPTANCE AS
        CODE describing exactly how it behaves.

        Notice the network call is still split into two `When` cases: the trigger
        (`userTappedIncrementButton`) and the result (`networkPostCompleted(DTO)`). Effects
        never bypass the update contract — an effect can only ever report back as another
        `When`.

        @Code(name: "CloudCounterTests.swift", file: StateWhenAndEffects-Reducer-CloudCounterTests-02.swift)
      }

      @Step {
        Define the `State` struct. Same two properties as the classic version —
        `viewDisplaysTotalCount` and the loading flag — just gathered into a single struct
        instead of scattered `@Published` properties.

        @Code(name: "CloudCounterReducer.swift", file: StateWhenAndEffects-Reducer-CloudCounter-02.swift)
      }

      @Step {
        Add the `When` cases. `networkPostCompleted(DTO)` is the completion event our
        effect will eventually send back.

        @Code(name: "CloudCounterReducer.swift", file: StateWhenAndEffects-Reducer-CloudCounter-03.swift)
      }

      @Step {
        Now the full implementation. `update` takes an extra `effectsState: inout
        EffectsState<When>` parameter — that's where effects get enqueued instead of a
        bare `enqueue(...)` self-call, since a static function has no `self`. Otherwise the
        shape is identical: mutate state synchronously, enqueue the async work, and let its
        completion arrive as another `When`.

        @Code(name: "CloudCounterReducer.swift", file: StateWhenAndEffects-Reducer-CloudCounter-04.swift)
      }

      Nice — a Reducer with effects, same acceptance criteria as the classic version, same
      behavior, no `self` anywhere.
    }
  }

  @Section(title: "Typed effects") {
    @ContentAndMedia {
      The classic tutorial's typed-effects section — a dedicated `Effect` type for
      testability, cancellability, and observability — applies to the Reducer pattern
      unchanged: `effectsState.enqueue(...)` accepts any `Effect`, typed or anonymous, the
      same way `enqueue(...)` does on a classic `Statostore`.
    }
    @Steps {
      @Step {
        There's no dedicated Reducer example for typed effects yet — for now, see
        <doc:01-02-StateWhenAndEffects>'s "Typed effects" section for the full walkthrough
        (`NetworkEffect`, cancellation, and testing enqueued effects). Everything there
        transfers directly: swap `enqueue(...)` for `effectsState.enqueue(...)` and
        `\.viewDisplaysTotalCount` for `\.state.viewDisplaysTotalCount` in tests.

        @Code(name: "CloudCounterReducer.swift", file: StateWhenAndEffects-Reducer-CloudCounter-04.swift)
      }
    }
  }
}
